      "message": "I\u0027m wondering if we actually understand the problem...\n\nDid you look at how both `created_vcpus` and `online_vcpus` are used in kvm_vm_ioctl_create_vcpu()? There is in fact almost no difference between them. They both are incremented when creating a vCPU, and never decremented after that. The differences between them are described in `struct kvm` definition in include/linux/kvm_host.h:\n\n```\n\t/*\n\t * created_vcpus is protected by kvm-\u003elock, and is incremented\n\t * at the beginning of KVM_CREATE_VCPU.  online_vcpus is only\n\t * incremented after storing the kvm_vcpu pointer in vcpus,\n\t * and is accessed atomically.\n\t */\n```\n\nso in other words, basically the only differences are: 1. `online_vcpus` is safe to use without taking the kvm-\u003elock mutex. 2. `online_vcpus` is incremented slightly later in kvm_vm_ioctl_create_vcpu().\n\nAlso please note that `created_vcpus` used by pKVM in  arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/trace.c is a different `created_vcpus`, it is pKVM\u0027s one, not the host\u0027s one. It is incremented by pKVM when the host makes the vcpu_create hypercall.\n\nSo we have even 3 different counters of vCPUs, not just 2.\n\nSo, IIUC the order of actions during a vCPU creation in kvm_vm_ioctl_create_vcpu() is:\n1. increment the host\u0027s `created_vcpus` (under kvm-\u003elock)\n2. increment pKVM\u0027s `created_vcpus` (in pKVM while handling `vcpu_create` hypercall issued in kvm_arch_vcpu_create() -\u003e kvm_x86_call(vcpu_create)() -\u003e pkvm_vcpu_create())\n3. increment `online_vcpus`\n\nSo it _seems_ plausible that the observed race (causing insufficient size of the vmexit trace buffer) is indeed due to pKVM\u0027s `created_vcpus` already incremented, while host\u0027s `online_vcpus` not incremented yet. And then indeed it can be avoided by using host\u0027s `created_vcpus` instead of `online_vcpus` when allocating the vmexit trace buffer (since host\u0027s `created_vcpus` is incremented before pKVM\u0027s `created_vcpus`, not after).\n\nBut does that really fully prevent the race? What if the host\u0027s and pKVM\u0027s `created_vcpus` are also incremented in the meantime by another thread on another CPU, right after the host allocates the vmexit trace buffer but before pKVM handles the `dump_vmexit_trace` hypercall using this buffer? Then we have the same race?\n\n(And this scenario actually seems no less likely, since the VMM usually creates vCPUs in parallel in different threads - in those exact threads in which those vCPUs will run.)\n\nSo this race seems generally inevitable (unless we introduce another, coarser-graned per-VM lock, just for this purpose)?\n\nAnd this race is actually not a big problem anyway, it only results in failing to dump the whole vmexit trace data?\n\nAnd BTW I suppose the reason why Chuanxiao didn\u0027t originally add propagation of that -ENOSPC error to the host is to let the host \"successfully\" print at least a part of the vmexit data - that part that fits in the buffer?\n\nAnd since this is just a rare and momentary condition, when the VM\u0027s vCPU are still only being created, perhaps this is not an issue at all? Who cares if, for example, for a VM with 8 vCPUs, only 3 out of 5 _already created_ vCPUs are dumped when we dump vmexit trace while the VM is only being started?",
